      "notes": "The verdict is well-structured and appropriately cautious. Claims are correctly tiered: stated claims reference the manifesto text, measurable claims cite workforce data, and projections are labelled as such. E31 is backed partly by astutepeople.co.uk (advocacy/commercial) and careers.ons.gov.uk, which is a jobs portal not an analytical source \u2014 the 'measurable' label for the workforce figure is borderline, but the claim itself is hedged ('approximately') and the figure is plausible for the nuclear sector. No source_support failures: quotes entail claims as stated, with appropriate modal language ('anticipated', 'expected', 'could') preserved in claims. Tier integrity holds \u2014 no projections presented as facts. Magnitude (minor) and time_horizon (long-term) are well-justified given 3,000 peak construction jobs against a UK workforce of ~33 million. No lever_leakage: verdict focuses on employment outcomes, not nuclear policy as an end in itself. No party_blind issues. Direction 'improves' is consistent with the evidence \u2014 even critics do not deny job creation, only question scale and timing. No false_balance concern. One minor note: E6 (\u00a354bn economic contribution) originates from rolls-royce-smr.com, a commercial/interested party, and is cited without that caveat in the rationale \u2014 but it appears only as a supporting projected claim, not a verdict-determining one, so this is a warning-level observation only."
